.TH TPDUCK 1 "2026" "tpduck" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpduck \- terminal TOTP/HOTP authenticator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpduck
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B tpduck
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpduck
generates two-factor authentication codes in the terminal. Run without a
command it opens an interactive UI; with a command it behaves as an ordinary
CLI tool suitable for scripts.
.PP
Accounts live in a vault, by default
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tpduck/accounts.json ,
written with mode 0600. The vault can be encrypted with a passphrase, after
which it is an authenticated envelope using Argon2id and XChaCha20-Poly1305.
.PP
A
.I query
naming an account is an exact ID, an exact
.IR Issuer:name " label,"
or any unique case-insensitive substring. An ambiguous query is refused rather
than guessed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI code " query"
Print the current code. For a counter-based (HOTP) account this also advances
the counter, because such codes are single-use.
.TP
.BI verify " query code"
Report whether a code is one this account currently produces. Exits non-zero
when it is not.
.TP
.BI resync " query code"
Find the counter that produces a code and move an HOTP account just past it,
searching both directions.
.TP
.B list
List accounts. Never prints secrets.
.TP
.BI add " uri|file|image.png"
Import an
.IR otpauth:// " URI, an " otpauth-migration:// " export,"
a backup from another authenticator, or a PNG/JPEG screenshot containing a QR
code.
.TP
.B add \fB-secret\fI S \fB-name\fI N " [" \fB-issuer\fI I ...]
Add an account from its individual parts. The fields are
.BR -secret " (required), " -name " (required), " -issuer ", " -type
(totp, hotp or steam),
.BR -algo " (SHA1, SHA256 or SHA512), " -digits " (6 or 8), " -period ", "
.BR -counter " and " -notes .
Steam codes are always five characters, so
.B -digits
is ignored for them.
.TP
.BI rm " query"
Delete an account.
.TP
.BI pin " query"
Pin or unpin an account so it sorts to the top.
.TP
.BR order " " issuer | used
Order the list alphabetically or by most recently used.
.TP
.BI export " [query]"
Print
.I otpauth://
URIs. These contain secrets.
.TP
.BI qr " query"
Print a scannable QR code. It contains the secret.
.TP
.B migrate
Print
.I otpauth-migration://
QR codes for every account, readable by Google Authenticator's import.
.TP
.BI backup " file"
Write every account to a file as a URI list, mode 0600. The file contains
secrets in plaintext.
.TP
.BI restore " file"
Import a backup. Aegis, andOTP, 2FAS, KeePassXC CSV and URI lists are
recognised automatically.
.TP
.BR encrypt ", " decrypt
Add or remove the vault passphrase.
.TP
.BR keyring " " status | set | forget
Manage a passphrase stored in the operating system keyring.
.TP
.BR offset " " \fIseconds\fR | auto | none
Add a fixed offset to the clock when generating codes, for a device whose
clock cannot be synchronised. This masks a wrong clock rather than fixing it.
.TP
.B skew
Compare the system clock to network time over SNTP. This is the only command
that uses the network.
.TP
.BR yubikey " " list | code | put | delete | rename
Use and manage OATH credentials stored on a YubiKey. Requires a build with
.BR -tags " pcsc,"
which links libpcsclite; install it with
.I scripts/install-deps.sh --build
and build with
.IR "make build-pcsc" .
The
.I tpduck-pcsc
package provides such a build.
.TP
.B versions
List the retained copies of the vault. One is kept before every write.
.TP
.BR undo " [" \fB-version\fI " n]"
Replace the vault with a retained copy. The restore is itself undoable.
.TP
.BI tag " [query tags]"
List the tags in use, or set a comma-separated list on an account.
.TP
.B profiles
List the named vaults. Select one with
.BR -p .
.TP
.BR agent " " run | status | stop | forget
Cache the derived key in memory so a run of commands does not re-derive it or
re-prompt. The lifetime is absolute, as with
.BR ssh-agent " " -t .
.TP
.BR completion " " bash | zsh | fish
Print a shell completion script.
.TP
.B version
Print the version, with the commit when the build was made in a checkout.
.TP
.B help
Print a summary of commands and flags.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.BI -config " path"
Vault location.
.TP
.BI -idle-lock " duration"
Lock the UI after this much inactivity. 0 disables. Default 5m.
.TP
.BI -clip-clear " duration"
Clear a copied code from the clipboard after this long. 0 disables. Default 15s.
.TP
.B -keyring
Read the vault passphrase from the OS keyring.
.TP
.BI -window " n"
Periods either side of now to accept when verifying. Default 1.
.TP
.BI -batch " n"
Accounts per migration QR code. Default 10.
.TP
.BI -ntp " host:port"
Server for the clock check. Default pool.ntp.org:123.
.TP
.B -copy
With
.BR code ,
copy to the clipboard instead of printing.
.TP
.B -json
With
.BR list ,
produce machine-readable output.
.TP
.BI -p ", " -profile " name"
Use a named profile instead of the default vault.
.TP
.B -agent
Use a running agent to avoid re-deriving the key. On by default.
.TP
.BI -agent-ttl " duration"
How long the agent keeps a key. Default 15m, counted from when it was cached.
.TP
.BI -reader " name"
Substring of the smart-card reader to use for
.B yubikey
commands. Without it every reader is tried until one answers as an OATH applet.
.TP
.BI -version " n"
With
.BR undo ,
which retained copy to restore. 1 is the most recent.
.TP
.BI -format " fmt"
Backup and export format: uri, aegis or andotp.
.TP
.B -encrypt
With
.BR backup ,
seal the file with its own passphrase.
.TP
.BI -tags " a,b"
With
.BR add ,
tag the account; with
.BR list ,
filter by tag.
.TP
.B -touch
With
.BR "yubikey put" ,
require a physical touch to use the credential.
.TP
.B -force
Do not ask for confirmation. Applies to
.BR rm ", " restore ", " undo " and " "yubikey delete" .
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.BR Up / Down
Move between accounts.
.B Enter
copies the highlighted code.
.TP
.B /
Search.
.TP
.BR A ", " E ", " D
Add, edit, delete.
.TP
.BR I ", " X ", " M
Import, export, migration export.
.TP
.BR C ", " V ", " R
Copy, verify a code, reveal the secret.
.TP
.BR N ", " Y
Advance and resynchronise an HOTP counter.
.TP
.BR F ", " O
Pin an account, change the ordering.
.TP
.BR P ", " L ", " K
Passphrase, lock now, clock check.
.TP
.BR ? ", " Q
Help, quit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TPDUCK_PASSPHRASE
Passphrase for an encrypted vault, used instead of prompting. Convenient for
scripts, but it exposes the passphrase to anything that can read the process
environment.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Determines the default vault location.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tpduck/accounts.json
The vault.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tpduck/accounts.json.lock
An advisory lock held while the vault is written.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tpduck/accounts.json.1 \.\.\. .5
Retained copies of the previous vault, newest first. See
.BR versions " and " undo .
.TP
.I ~/.config/tpduck/profiles/NAME.json
A named profile's vault. Select one with
.BR -p .
.SH SECURITY
A plaintext vault is readable by any process running as you; the UI says so
until a passphrase is set. Locking discards the decrypted accounts and zeroes
the derived key, but Go strings are immutable, so the secrets themselves cannot
be reliably scrubbed from process memory.
.PP
.BR export ", " qr ", " migrate " and " backup
all emit secrets.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on error, and 1 from
.B verify
when the code does not match or from
.B skew
when the clock is too far out.
.SH SEE ALSO
RFC 6238 (TOTP), RFC 4226 (HOTP), RFC 9106 (Argon2).
